---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site';
import { link } from '../lib/url';

const initials = SITE.author
  .split(' ')
  .map((part) => part[0])
  .join('');
---

<Base title="About" description={SITE.authorBio}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="meta">About</p>
      <h1>Hello, I am Odette.</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">I make small websites for small businesses, and I write down what I learn here.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="page-body with-margin">
      <div class="monogram" aria-hidden="true">{initials}</div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I have been building websites for fourteen years, the last six on my own. My clients are bookshops, bakeries,
          a harbor ferry and one very patient beekeeper. None of them want a web application. They want a page that
          loads, says the right thing and lets a customer get in touch.
        </p>
        <p>
          That turns out to be a craft with real depth. Choosing a line length, wording a form label, deciding what to
          leave out: these are small decisions, and a good site is mostly made of them.
        </p>

        <h2>What this notebook is</h2>
        <p>
          Galley is where I keep notes on that craft. A galley is the long tray a printer sets type in, and the rough
          proof pulled from it before anything is final. The posts here are in that spirit. They are short, practical
          and open to correction.
        </p>

        <h2>Get in touch</h2>
        <p>
          If you have a question, a correction or a site that needs making, use the{' '}
          <a href={link('/contact/')}>contact page</a>. I answer everything, usually within a couple of days.
        </p>

        <p class="colophon">
          Colophon: set in Newsreader and Bricolage Grotesque. Built with Astro as plain static files. Forms are handled
          by <a href="https://formtango.com">FormTango</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
